{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310712,"nonce":"pZj4UCOvIVBwmfgZQ1yvbz1KhWI=","title":"Haxe statics had separate storage per backend, so a static written by Cranelift read as null in promoted LLVM code — the deltablue signal 11","message":"**FIXED.** A Haxe `static var` had a different home in each execution tier:\n\n| tier | storage |\n|---|---|\n| MIR interpreter | `MirInterpreter::global_store`, a map of NanBoxed values |\n| Cranelift | the runtime's table, via `rayzor_global_load` / `rayzor_global_store` |\n| LLVM | a **module-private, zero-initialised `Internal` global**, inlined — no runtime call at all |\n\nNothing forced them to agree, so any handover between tiers lost every static.\nThat is the long-standing **deltablue `signal 11` at the Maximum tier**:\n`Strength.REQUIRED` and its six siblings are written by `__init__` running\nunder Cranelift, and promoted LLVM code read its own zeroed copy — null. The\ncrash is `mov 0x8(%rax),%eax` with `rax = 0`, a null field read, in `satisfy`.\n\n**Why it looked like something else.** Pure `rayzor-llvm` runs deltablue fine\n(16.6 ms), because it compiles `__init__` too and the module is consistent with\nitself. Only *promotion mid-run* crosses the boundary. Three facts pointed at a\nhandover problem and I read them as a bad call target until I disassembled:\ncrash only after promotion, pure-LLVM fine, `rax = 0`.\n\n**Do not reason about this as \"one module per drain\".** That framing is wrong\nand cost two mistaken write-ups: ONE LLVM module is enough to break it, the\nmoment it runs after another tier initialised anything.\n\nThe fix, in `runtime/src/lib.rs` and `llvm_jit_backend.rs`:\n- The runtime's store is now chunked, leaked, and **address-stable**, with a\n  lock-free read path (`AtomicPtr` chunk table, allocated on first use). It is\n  also **process-wide** rather than `thread_local` — which is correct Haxe\n  semantics and fixes a separate latent bug where a spawned thread saw zeros.\n- `rayzor_global_slot(id) -\u003e *mut u64` hands out a slot address.\n- LLVM **declares** each static (`External`, no initializer) and binds it with\n  `add_global_mapping` once an engine exists. It keeps its inlined load — still\n  faster than Cranelift's call — while both read one storage.\n- AOT keeps the private definition: the whole program is compiled together, so\n  it is consistent with itself and there is no runtime to bind to.\n\nSTILL SEPARATE: the interpreter's `global_store`. It does not bite today\nbecause the tiered path runs `__init__` through compiled code, never the\ninterpreter — but it is the same divergence and will bite if that changes.\n\nSee [[project_per_function_llvm_promotion]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_statics_live_in_three_places` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}